            <ArticleTitle>Isolated hydatid cyst of the diaphragm, a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>130-132</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(16)30469-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2016.10.071</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hydatid disease mainly affect lung and liver. We report a very rare case of hydatid cyst of the diaphragm.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT INFORMATION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A 25-year-old female presented with vague right hypochondrial pain for 2 months. Clinical Findings showed mild tenderness at right hypochondrial region, Ultrasound showed segment 7 hepatic cystic lesion of about 5×6×7 centimeters, relatively thick wall, with eccentrically coarse calcification. Computed tomography showed cystic lesion affecting segment 7 of the liver with rims of calcification, appearance is consistent with hydatid cyst of the liver. At laparascopy the liver was found to be normal with a bulging from the lateral border of the diaphragm. Postero-lateral mini-thoracotomy was performed and cyst was found to be inside. Resection was done with direct repair of the diaphragm. The histopathogical examination confirmed hydatid cyst of the diaphragm.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Isolated hydatid cyst of the diaphragm is a very rare entity. Pre-operative diagnosis is mandatory to prevent unnecessary excessive incisions.</AbstractText>
